WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.319 --> 00:00:05.610
BRETT HESTERBERG: First off,
thank you for walking over

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.650
from Moscone, many of you.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:11.700
I'm very happy to be talking
today about Google's newest

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.530
managed database offering.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.950
Cloud SQL, as of today,
is announcing support

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.650
for Postgres, which has
been one of our most

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:20.880
requested enhancements.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.680
So it's nice to be
able to answer yes

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.530
to that question starting today.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.280
What we're going to
talk about, of course,

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.370
is Cloud SQL for Postgres.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.009
I'll do some focusing
on certain features that

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.870
are in the beta product.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.760
And then we'll also look ahead.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.010
Cloud SQL for Postgres
is a beta product,

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.669
which means we're
just getting started.

00:00:39.669 --> 00:00:41.460
You're going to see us
deliver new features

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.170
during the beta program.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.170
And I'll talk about
a few of those today.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.270
After that, we'll
invite Tim Kelton,

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.200
who is co-founder and cloud
architect at Descartes Labs.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.560
Tim has been an early adopter
of Cloud SQL for Postgres,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.930
and generally has used
Postgres for many years

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.870
to build some really stunning
geospatial applications.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.700
Tim will walk us
through how Descartes

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.580
is using Google Cloud to
build these applications,

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.217
and hopefully show
off one as well.

00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:10.300
And then we'll get into
some next steps and things

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.520
that hopefully
you can take away.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.600
Before we jump into all of that,
I have a few questions for you.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.180
By show of hands, how many
of you in the audience

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.100
are developers?

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.250
So we got-- call it 2/3.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.760
How about database
administrators?

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.490
How about reluctant
database administrators?

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.720
There's a few more hands.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.110
And lastly, any managers
in the audience?

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.260
One brave soul, maybe two.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.190
Thank you.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.430
How many of you are
using Postgres today,

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.625
or use it frequently?

00:01:45.625 --> 00:01:46.750
That's about half the room.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.291
And how many of you
in the audience--

00:01:48.291 --> 00:01:49.480
last question here for now--

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.890
are Cloud SQL users?

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:53.850
Not nearly as many.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:54.450
OK, great.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.616
Well, I think we're going
to have a little something

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.350
for everyone in the audience.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.300
And we'll jump right in.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.230
Let me start by saying
that Cloud SQL is

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.430
launching Postgres.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.080
And when I say that, if you're
all on your laptops right now

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.130
and you go to Cloud
Console, click on Cloud SQL.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.380
You won't see Postgres
get in the Console.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.120
We're launching it
as we speak, which

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.365
means some of you,
today and tomorrow,

00:02:17.365 --> 00:02:18.990
will begin to see
that in your Console.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.940
Many of you, over
this coming weekend,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.200
will see it in your Console.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.570
And all of you, by
early next week,

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.220
will see Postgres
in your Console,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.690
and be able to create instances.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.290
That said, all of you today,
with the CLI or an API,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.920
can create a Cloud SQL for
Postgres instance right now.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.220
So, based on the
response from the room,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.710
I don't have to go into
the merits of Postgres.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.040
But I think many of you
using Postgres right now see

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.730
that it's a powerful open
source relational database.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.160
Really, a vibrant open
source community backing it,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.620
with many years of
active development.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.730
And in addition to strong
standards compliance,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.310
I think one of the most
unique features about Postgres

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.556
is the fact that
it is extensible.

00:03:01.556 --> 00:03:03.180
And we'll be talking
today specifically

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.250
about one extension,
PostGIS, which

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.130
adds powerful object
or geospatial object

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.600
support to Postgres.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.500
So where does Cloud SQL fit
in Google's fairly broad

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.050
and growing database portfolio?

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.560
On this slide, rightmost,
you see Google's data

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.450
warehousing product, BigQuery--

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:23.980
again, rightmost column.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.300
We store objects in
Cloud Storage or GCS.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:32.350
We have non-relational NoSQL
databases in Cloud Datastore

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:33.312
and BigTable.

00:03:33.312 --> 00:03:35.020
Finally, you get to
the relational space,

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.961
where Cloud SQL lives
along with Spanner.

00:03:36.961 --> 00:03:38.710
And if you haven't
taken a look at Spanner

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:40.084
yet, or been to
some of the talks

00:03:40.084 --> 00:03:41.890
about Spanner going
on at the conference,

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.932
I would encourage you to do so.

00:03:44.932 --> 00:03:46.390
And then furthest
over on the left,

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:50.950
we have our in memory products
in Memcache for App Engine.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:55.120
So you get kind of a feel for
where Cloud SQL is, broadly,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.260
in the database portfolio.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.480
The thing I'll note
about Cloud SQL

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.770
is that Cloud SQL
delivers standard MySQL

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.510
and now Postgres databases.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.420
Which means if you have
an application running

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.120
with Postgres today,
with few exceptions

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.430
your application will run
immediately with Cloud SQL.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.890
We're not offering Postgres
compatibility layers,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.610
we're offering standard
Postgres and standard MySQL.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:22.450
The other thing I'll say about
Cloud SQL is it is not a DBA.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:26.890
We do our best to offload
the more mundane database

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:28.990
administration tasks--
installing databases,

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:31.540
patching them,
setting up backups--

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.180
but we are not a
DBA In the sense

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.390
that Cloud SQL won't
optimize queries, et cetera.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:42.610
As we look at running databases
in Google Cloud, or on-prem,

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.590
or elsewhere, I think
the thing you'll

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.420
hear me repeat in
this presentation

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:48.980
is Cloud SQL is a
managed database service.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:50.710
It's important, I
think, to distinguish

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:52.160
what exactly that means.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.820
So let's do this by just
taking a look at what

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:56.890
it takes to run a database.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.766
And we'll start with
the hardware layer.

00:04:58.766 --> 00:05:00.640
Folks in the audience
who are running on-prem

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.514
are thinking today
about power and cooling.

00:05:03.514 --> 00:05:04.930
Even if you're
not, even if you've

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.930
gone to a co-lo or a hosting
provider or a cloud vendor,

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.660
I would encourage you to think
about server maintenance.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.660
Here in Google Cloud, part
of our server maintenance

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:16.240
is live migration
with Compute Engine.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:17.930
Cloud SQL benefits
from that feature.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.717
And if you aren't
aware of it, there's

00:05:19.717 --> 00:05:22.300
been a few great blog posts put
out by the Compute Engine team

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.650
on live migration.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.140
Obviously, server maintenance,
whether you are maintaining

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.780
your servers or not,
has major implications

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:30.517
for your application stack.

00:05:30.517 --> 00:05:33.100
So then we look at the operating
system on top of the servers.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.864
And this is where we
reach a fork in the road.

00:05:35.864 --> 00:05:37.780
This is the first place
where if I'm deciding,

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.020
should I self-host a database
on Google Cloud, for example,

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.600
or go to a managed database
products like Cloud SQL?

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.850
We start to see some
real differences.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:47.309
If you're self-managing
a database,

00:05:47.309 --> 00:05:49.224
you're going to install
your operating system,

00:05:49.224 --> 00:05:51.520
you're going to maintain
it with patches, et cetera.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.810
Cloud SQL, you won't see
the operating system.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.040
We maintain it for you.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.481
It's not even exposed.

00:05:56.481 --> 00:05:58.480
Of course, when you're
self-managing a database,

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.250
you're going to do
the database install.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.125
You're going to patch
it, keep it up to date.

00:06:02.125 --> 00:06:03.820
You're going to set
up database backups.

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:06.070
In the Cloud SQL or
the managed world,

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:07.660
we're doing that,
again, for you.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.300
We're turning on automatic
backups if you'd like us to.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.280
We're pruning those
as we go along.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.600
And we've also taken care of
the software patches, et cetera.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.050
Lastly, we'll look at
the advanced features,

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:21.050
high availability.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.600
If you're self-managing,
to get high availability,

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.860
you are setting up replication
between Postgres databases,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:27.790
for example.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:29.320
You've built tooling
that monitors

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.270
the health of these databases.

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:33.550
And you are alerting so
that you can manually

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.920
failover in the
case of some outage,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.020
or you've built additional
scripts, et cetera,

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:40.090
to failover automatically.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:42.490
In the managed database
space, with Cloud SQL,

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.220
Cloud SQL takes care of
high availability for you

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.380
in that it sets up
the replication.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:50.050
We do the monitoring and
actually trigger the failovers.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.600
The other thing here is,
in the managed world,

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.060
you are backed by Google's team.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.940
In the self-managed world,
you are backing the database

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.190
availability itself.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.410
Last item on the
slide is scaling.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:04.630
In a self-managed
world, you decide

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.660
when to scale up, when to scale
out, and you implement those.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:08.649
In the managed world--

00:07:08.649 --> 00:07:09.940
want to make special note here.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:12.520
Cloud SQL helps you
scale more easily.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:14.950
We help you scale up easily,
help you scale out easily.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:19.380
But unlike other database
products in our portfolio,

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.380
Cloud SQL does not auto scale.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.410
So it's up to you still,
as an administrator,

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.520
to decide it's time to scale
up, or it's time to scale out.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.800
One thing that we shouldn't
forget is monitoring.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.410
And monitoring goes beyond
just application-level kind

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.300
of monitoring,
Stackdriver for example.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.900
And this can be something
like, I want to make sure

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:42.370
that my slow query logs get to
Pub/Sub or to Cloud Logging,

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:46.210
for easy ingestion by third
party apps like Splunk,

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:46.854
for example.

00:07:46.854 --> 00:07:48.770
With a managed service,
a lot of this plumbing

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:49.570
is set up for you.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:51.010
Self-managed, it's not.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.194
Now, I'm the Cloud
SQL product manager,

00:07:53.194 --> 00:07:54.610
so obviously I
have some bias here

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.200
in terms of managed databases.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.690
My point here is not to
say that one is necessarily

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:00.130
better than the other.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:02.422
You all are making
tradeoffs during your day.

00:08:02.422 --> 00:08:03.880
You're deciding,
I'm going to spend

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:07.180
more time in this piece
of my technology stack,

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:10.540
to make sure it is customized
to exactly what I want.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:13.780
And as a trade-off, I'm going
to spend more time managing

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:15.010
that piece of the stack.

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:17.620
I think the decision between
self-managing databases

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:20.950
and a managed database service
like cloud SQL, is very much

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:21.640
that.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:23.680
You will lose customization
or flexibility

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:25.320
when you go to
the managed world,

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.400
but you gain all
these offloading

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.400
of what we call mundane tasks.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.660
The self-managed world,
you can tune Postgres

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:34.630
to your heart's content, but
you are managing that database,

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.143
and managing its up time.

00:08:36.143 --> 00:08:37.809
So hopefully that
gives you a good sense

00:08:37.809 --> 00:08:39.429
for how we break
down the difference

00:08:39.429 --> 00:08:42.017
between self-managing
and managing databases.

00:08:42.017 --> 00:08:44.350
And again, I'm happy to say
that Cloud SQL now gives you

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:47.090
the option to do
this with Postgres.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.870
So let me highlight just
a few features for you.

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:52.670
As a starting point,
Cloud SQL for Postgres

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:56.770
offers large instance
sizes, up to 32 cores

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.530
and more than 200
gigabytes of RAM.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.720
You can see in the
screenshot here

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.400
we are showing the
fact that we're

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:06.010
using custom VMs as the
underlying mechanism to Cloud

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:08.890
SQL, which means
you get granularity

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:12.010
with respect to number
of CPU cores, size

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.110
of RAM on your instance type.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:17.230
The other thing I'll note is,
we have inexpensive development

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:17.800
instances.

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:21.230
Our smallest instances start
at less than $10 a month.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:24.310
So you can get going with
Postgres very affordably,

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:25.960
for example.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:29.110
Next feature I'll
talk about is storage.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:32.380
I think it's intuitive for us
to scale a database in terms

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.720
of CPUs and RAM.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.067
A little less intuitive
is scaling performance

00:09:37.067 --> 00:09:37.900
in terms of storage.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:40.450
And the thing I'll note
here is that at Google, we

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:44.170
scale storage performance
by scaling capacity.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:47.350
So if you add capacity,
you get more performance.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:51.040
What you're seeing here, built
into the Cloud SQL instance

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:53.830
creation form that will be
coming live to your console

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:55.780
soon, is--

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:58.090
we see the performance
calculator on the bottom.

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:00.190
And what it's
showing you is this

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.530
is the performance
level you can expect

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:06.070
based on the number of
CPU cores you've selected,

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:07.840
and the size of your storage.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:09.430
In this case, you
can see I am not

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:12.290
maxing out the IOPS I could
get out of my instance.

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:14.380
If I were to add
capacity, you would

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:17.330
see that slider bar ratchet up
all the way to, in this case,

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:19.340
25,000 IOPS.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:22.936
There's a small checkbox next to
a one-liner in our Create form

00:10:22.936 --> 00:10:24.310
that I think is
really important.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.580
And it's our auto
storage increase feature.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:29.090
So when you check
this box, you can

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.450
see it enable the
auto storage increase.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:35.080
That tells Google, you deal
with my storage capacity.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.680
As I get near my 10-gigabyte
limit, or 400-gigabyte limit

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.650
in this case, increase
the storage capacity

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:43.370
for me automatically.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:46.630
And the nice thing about Google
persistent storage is that we

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:49.210
can increase the storage--
whether capacity, performance,

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:50.140
both--

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:51.010
as a hot operation.

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:52.426
You don't take
your database down.

00:10:52.426 --> 00:10:55.480
So if you check that box, you
leave the storage capacity

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:56.519
management to us.

00:10:56.519 --> 00:10:58.810
We will automatically grow
your storage as you need it,

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:01.920
while your database is running.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.440
Next feature is backups
that are included

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.270
with Cloud SQL for Postgres.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.790
You can enable automatic
backups, which get taken daily.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:12.300
You tell us what time of day
you want those backups taken.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:13.770
We automatically prune those.

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:16.620
After seven days, we'll get
rid of your oldest backup

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:17.690
and take the newest.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:20.710
So you get a rolling
seven-day automatic backup.

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:22.500
You can also take
on-demand backups.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.080
These are manual backups
that you control yourself.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:26.364
You decide when to take them.

00:11:26.364 --> 00:11:27.780
And you decide how
to retain them.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.480
If you want to retain one for a
year, or three years, or seven

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:31.650
years, you can do so.

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:33.862
We keep them as
long as you want.

00:11:33.862 --> 00:11:35.070
With respect to maintenance--

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:37.800
I talked about maintenance
in that decision

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.010
between self-managing
and managing database.

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.690
With respect to maintenance,
we offer a maintenance window.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.504
You tell us day of
week and time of day,

00:11:46.504 --> 00:11:48.420
and we'll make sure that
any maintenance we do

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:50.730
is performed in that window.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:52.840
You also tell us
maintenance timing.

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:56.190
So if you have two Postgres
database instances in a given

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:59.250
project, you might say, I would
like my development instance

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:03.360
to be maintained or updated
before my production instance.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.550
And you can use the
maintenance timing field here,

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:09.690
which is earlier and later,
to decide that my development

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.810
instance should be updated
before my production instance.

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:14.520
And when you do that,
you get yourself

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:17.070
about a week of difference
between these two things.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.260
So something on
the earlier track

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:23.100
will get updated one week before
something on the later track,

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:27.830
is our general guidance
on maintenance timing.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.530
The last point I'll
mention here is extensions.

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:33.620
And I think part of
Postgres' uniqueness

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:38.210
and its power is in its
extension ecosystem.

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:42.267
We've included a handful of
extensions at the beta launch.

00:12:42.267 --> 00:12:43.850
We expect this list
to grow over time,

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:45.420
and we'll look
for your feedback.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.410
The extension we wanted to make
sure we got into the product

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:50.680
for launch is PostGIS.

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:54.290
It's one of the most frequently
used extensions for Postgres,

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:56.720
and it's always used
by folks like yourself

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:00.536
who are interested in
geospatial applications.

00:13:00.536 --> 00:13:01.160
So what's next?

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:03.990
I told you that this
is a beta product

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:05.430
and we're just getting started.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:08.130
It means you're going to
see us land large features

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:10.830
during the beta period.

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:12.060
So what's not included today?

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:15.030
Things around replication
are not included today.

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:17.700
So for example, we don't
yet offer high availability,

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.430
nor do we offer read scale
out with read replicas.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.050
Those things are coming.

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.450
We offer backups and
recovery, but we don't yet

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:25.740
offer point in time recovery.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:27.480
Again, that's another
one that's coming.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.640
And from a connectivity
point of view,

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:32.310
today Cloud SQL for Postgres
supports connections

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:36.240
from Compute Engine, from App
Engine, flexible environment,

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:40.020
which just went GA, from
Container Engine, or generally

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:43.590
from any client that supports
a standard Postgres connector.

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:46.080
That said, we don't yet
support connectivity

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:50.340
from Cloud Functions, which was
announced at this conference.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:51.810
Nor do we get
support connectivity

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:53.060
from App Engine Standard.

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:54.810
So you've got a few
things to look forward

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:57.226
to as part of this beta period,
and to keep in mind as you

00:13:57.226 --> 00:13:59.170
start to use the product.

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:01.500
So I have another set
of questions for you.

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:05.010
And I will ask you
all, by show of hands--

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:07.410
and we'll be on the honor
system, you get one vote.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:10.980
If you're telling
us what to do next,

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:15.480
which of these five features
is most important to you?

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:18.060
So again, honor system,
you get one vote.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:19.890
How many of you say,
I want you, Google,

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:21.535
to do high availability next?

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:26.340
Got about-- call
it 1/3 of the room.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:27.798
All right, how
about read replicas?

00:14:27.798 --> 00:14:30.420
This is read scale out?

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:33.010
I'm counting Tim's vote as well.

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:34.780
Another 1/3 of the room.

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:36.475
Connectivity from
App Engine Standard?

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:40.390
One brave soul.

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:42.970
Cloud Functions connectivity?

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:43.730
A couple of folks.

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:45.605
A couple of folks are
excited about that one.

00:14:45.605 --> 00:14:48.234
And lastly, point
in time recovery?

00:14:48.234 --> 00:14:50.650
I'm going to assume all of you
are worried about recovery,

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:54.220
but are just not voting it
above these other features.

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:56.500
OK, so it looks like
the replication features

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:57.776
were our hottest commodity.

00:14:57.776 --> 00:14:58.900
I appreciate that feedback.

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:01.930
And if you do have questions as
we go along, keep them in mind.

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:03.370
Tim and I are going to
leave some time at the end,

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:04.828
and we've got mics
so that we'll be

00:15:04.828 --> 00:15:06.790
able to answer your questions.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:09.700
So, it's one thing to
talk just about a product.

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:11.680
I think where this
gets exciting is

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:13.570
how you put a product
like this together

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:16.990
with other technologies to
build a great application.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:19.960
And I'm excited to say
that Tim Kelton, who

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:23.650
is the co-founder of Descartes
Labs, is here with us today.

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:26.290
Tim and Descartes Labs
have been an early adopter

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:27.550
of Cloud SQL for Postgres.

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:30.700
They participated in a
private alpha program.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.280
Tim and the team at
Descartes have put together

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:36.460
Postgres with PostGIS
and a number of products

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:39.370
in Google Cloud to build
some outstanding geospatial

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:40.540
applications.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:43.150
And I ask you all to
join me in welcoming Tim.

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:50.820
TIM KELTON: Thanks, Brett.

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:54.030
Today, I'd like to share with
you how some of the geospatial

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:56.460
features that Brett
just mentioned

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.970
are helping us at Descartes
Labs focus on the really

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:03.960
hard problems we're trying
to solve, and less on the day

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:08.250
to day managing and
scaling of infrastructure.

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:13.560
So, what are those
hard problems?

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:17.820
Descartes Labs applies
machine learning and AI

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:20.970
to global satellite
and aerial imagery

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:24.060
to see how the Earth
changes every day.

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:28.650
And our goal is to help
customers quantify and predict

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:31.470
how those changes
will affect them.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:34.110
And our first products
looked at things like,

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:37.720
how much food are we
producing in North America?

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:42.470
And we built a platform
on Google Cloud,

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:45.950
initially to answer those
questions such as agriculture.

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:49.070
But we've since found as
a range of applications

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:52.220
in areas such as
security and trading,

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:54.530
but also in supply
chain and logistics.

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:05.859
So, Descartes Labs was
founded in December of 2014.

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:09.280
We are Silicon Valley backed,
and we have Silicon Valley

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:10.750
veteran leadership.

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:13.060
And that's where
the story changes.

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:15.770
We're not the stereotypical
Silicon Valley startup.

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:19.000
Myself and the rest
of the founding team

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:22.569
were researchers,
engineers, and scientists

00:17:22.569 --> 00:17:25.780
high up in the mountains of
northern New Mexico at Los

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:28.060
Alamos National Laboratory.

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:33.370
And there we focused on
areas such as deep learning,

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:38.590
remote sensing, and large-scale
high performance computing.

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:41.020
And today we have
nearly 30 employees,

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:44.870
and relevant to this talk,
we have no DBAs on staff.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:49.990
So, how did we
find Google Cloud?

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:53.170
It's a little bit different
story than maybe typically.

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:56.560
As an early-stage startup, we
kind of just go spend millions

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:59.470
and millions of dollars
buying satellite imagery,

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:02.770
and the Earth Engine team
at Google actually provided

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:05.680
a public data set called--

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:07.900
from NASA-- called Landsat.

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:11.290
And Landsat was
launched in 1973,

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:15.330
and has been continuously
observing the Earth since then.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:17.350
And so that is
available natively

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:21.250
on Google Cloud Storage,
in a Cloud Storage bucket.

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:25.210
You can just simply GSUtil
LS and see all of the imagery

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:26.050
there.

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:28.780
So we started, in the early
months of the company,

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:32.650
started pulling imagery
down to VMs on Google Cloud,

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:37.150
and building small-scale
models over very

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:43.060
limited geographical regions and
over very small time windows.

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:46.900
Trying to do something like
forecast food production

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:47.860
in the United States.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:50.330
It was quickly apparent
that wouldn't work.

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:53.380
So we needed to scale
up our processing.

00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:55.450
Coming from places
like Los Alamos, where

00:18:55.450 --> 00:18:58.090
you have high performance
supercomputing

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:00.970
and access to a
lot of resources,

00:19:00.970 --> 00:19:03.490
you're not necessarily
instantly confident

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:05.200
that the Cloud
will actually scale

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:06.985
to the levels you need it to.

00:19:06.985 --> 00:19:09.220
And very quickly,
we knew we would

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:13.300
need to use models that would
cover large geographic regions

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:15.580
such as the entire
United States,

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:19.750
and would see many, many
years of crops growing.

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:22.330
You can think,
here in California,

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:24.890
like two years ago,
what was the story?

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:29.590
It was all droughts
and bleak water levels.

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:32.560
And this year we have reservoirs
dramatically overflowing.

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:35.320
So to build accuracy
in these models,

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:37.090
we needed a large window.

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:39.190
And so just a few
months into the company,

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:42.070
we were able to scale
up on Google Cloud

00:19:42.070 --> 00:19:47.320
our code to process that entire
petabyte Landsat and MODIS

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:52.960
imagery to process all of
that in just over 15 hours

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:55.570
on 30,000 virtual cores.

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:59.650
And that's the power of having
this data natively on the Cloud

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:01.630
right next to Compute.

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:05.770
And, like I said, coming from
places with high performance

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:07.960
computing, what we are
so pleasantly surprised

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:11.560
with was not just that we were
able to get lots of cores,

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:14.680
but also that storage
was able to scale

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:16.910
with our needs and networking.

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:18.640
All of those need
to scale uniformly

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:21.820
to actually scale up and process
that amount of data really

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:23.570
quickly.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:25.820
So, once we had processed
all of this data,

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:28.180
we were able to build
models over much

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:31.660
larger geographic regions such
as the whole United States,

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:35.080
and see daily changes as
new satellite imagery comes

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:37.060
in every single day.

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:40.812
In 2015, we had
our first models.

00:20:40.812 --> 00:20:42.520
And when they got
announced on Bloomberg,

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:44.395
they actually moved the
market a few percent.

00:20:44.395 --> 00:20:47.860
And we were all kind of
surprised and excited.

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:51.220
And then later in 2016,
as most people that

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:53.350
work in machine
learning are constantly

00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:56.440
trying to improve the
accuracy of the models,

00:20:56.440 --> 00:20:58.270
we improved our models.

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:00.340
We also started
working with customers

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:03.550
on larger geographic
regions around the Earth,

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:07.240
and on adding multiple, more--

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.460
multiple new crops.

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:12.160
Additionally, we
now actually release

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:15.850
our models, what we're
predicting as far as food

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:18.350
supply, on a mobile app.

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:22.810
So later in 2016,
on Google Cloud,

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:25.000
the Earth Engine team
made a second dataset

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:28.420
publicly available, again
natively on Cloud Storage.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:29.440
So this is just there.

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:31.270
You can go in your browser--

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:34.600
on your command line and
GSUtil LS this directory.

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:36.520
And that's from the
European Space Agency.

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:38.810
That's the Sentinel 2 data set.

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:41.320
So this is petabytes of
imagery that are actually

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:44.980
stored in Google Cloud, and
Google is paying the bill

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:46.996
for hosting this imagery.

00:21:46.996 --> 00:21:48.370
And so you can
quickly just start

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:51.880
working on the actual problem
you're trying to solve.

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:54.940
Today, I want to share with
you a little more information

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:59.350
about the platform we built
on top of Google Cloud.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:01.480
And I want to show you
a product that we just

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:04.870
launched on Tuesday called
Geovisual Search that we've

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:06.700
built on top of our platform.

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:14.590
So, just a high-level diagram of
what our platform consists of.

00:22:14.590 --> 00:22:18.180
As I already mentioned,
we have various data sets

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:21.780
from NASA and the US--

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:25.710
NASA, European Space Agency,
a number of other public data

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:26.670
sets.

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:28.810
And it's great when they
just live in the cloud.

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:31.620
But a lot of these we
actually need to pull in.

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:34.620
And the sensors
on satellites that

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:37.820
have been launched over
basically a decade or more

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:39.190
vary tremendously.

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:42.120
They vary in the amount of
light bands that they capture.

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:45.510
They vary in the sensor
technology that's used.

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:50.460
And our end goal is to make
a really common imagery API

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:54.120
that we can quickly have
our machine learning teams

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:56.360
have a nice Python API around.

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:58.260
And no matter which
set of imagery,

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:01.290
or which layers of
light, that it will

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:05.250
be a really uniform experience.

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:06.180
And so we have--

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:08.550
so you can think of
things more like a Python

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:12.450
API or a Jupyter Notebook
kind of similar to Datalab.

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:15.990
So our processing-- we
start with ingesting

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:17.860
all of this imagery.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:22.470
And we have to do things like co
register the imagery for really

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.010
accurate alignment.

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:27.120
We have to convert it
to standard projections.

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:28.590
Things like removing clouds.

00:23:28.590 --> 00:23:30.270
Here in San
Francisco, and I'm not

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:31.830
talking about
compute clouds, I'm

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:35.730
talking about overhead
clouds which in San Francisco

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:37.920
happen quite a bit.

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:42.750
Correct for shadows, adjust
for camera angles, angles

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:45.810
of the sun,
calibrate the sensors

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:49.670
against their neighbors,
atmospheric reflectance.

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:51.210
All of these things
have to happen

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:53.220
in our initial
processing pipeline.

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:56.130
And that happens
on Compute Engine,

00:23:56.130 --> 00:23:59.310
using primarily managed
instance groups.

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:02.460
And we heavily leverage
the preemptable instances

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:05.610
to cut down on
costs in that case,

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:10.240
because it's very embarrassingly
parallel workload.

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:16.120
So then, we have two directions
from our processing pipeline

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:17.660
where data goes.

00:24:17.660 --> 00:24:20.800
Our imagery, or what
we call raster data,

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:22.180
those are the pixels.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:23.570
That gets stored natively.

00:24:23.570 --> 00:24:25.480
I think, as Brett had
a slide, that gets

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:29.530
stored on Cloud Storage.

00:24:29.530 --> 00:24:32.440
We know that Cloud Storage
we will not run out of space.

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:35.320
We're expecting to have
nearly 15 petabytes of imagery

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:37.210
by the end of this year.

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:38.782
We know that it will scale.

00:24:38.782 --> 00:24:40.240
We know that we
can throw thousands

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:43.460
of cores processing against
it, and it will handle that.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:44.980
And it's nothing
we have to manage.

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:47.710
So all of our compressed
imagery gets stored.

00:24:47.710 --> 00:24:50.440
And this is on the
left side of the chart.

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:57.370
And then, those services are
available via microservices

00:24:57.370 --> 00:25:00.310
sitting on top of
Container Engine, where

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:04.780
we can query and pull down
those images really quickly.

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:09.280
The second part of our
imaging processing pipeline

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:12.040
is more of the vector data.

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:14.350
As we see each scene,
we capture pieces

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:16.450
of information about
that scene and we

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:21.400
use Pub/Sub to persist that
information into a PostGIS

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:22.070
database.

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:25.850
These are things we
need to spatially query.

00:25:25.850 --> 00:25:30.220
And so PostGIS enables us
a mechanism to do that,

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:34.480
and Cloud SQL supporting
PostGIS starts giving us

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:39.280
more of the freedom to not
do things like install GOS

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:43.930
and GDOL and Proj4J, and Lib
to XML, just to get things

00:25:43.930 --> 00:25:46.510
into a geospatial database.

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:49.720
And so those are more of
what we consider vector data.

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:53.860
Things like polygons and
points, or given points of data

00:25:53.860 --> 00:25:57.070
that we might use to train
our machine learning models.

00:25:57.070 --> 00:25:59.350
Say this given
geographic region,

00:25:59.350 --> 00:26:02.060
what do we see the values
at that given time?

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:05.390
And then try and train the
models to correlate with that.

00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:10.900
So we have many sources of
data that we store in PostGIS.

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:14.170
Things like public data
from maybe the US Department

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:15.160
of Agriculture.

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:17.610
But also private data sets
sometimes from customers

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:19.900
as well.

00:26:19.900 --> 00:26:22.500
So then, both of
those come together.

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:26.390
We host many, many
micro services.

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:29.170
Again, on top of either
managed instance groups

00:26:29.170 --> 00:26:31.780
or on top of GKE.

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:34.120
And then that's where
we are able to do

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:39.460
image searching, image rasters,
location based searches,

00:26:39.460 --> 00:26:42.170
and build models on top of that.

00:26:42.170 --> 00:26:43.510
And finally, one last theme.

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:46.630
In the last six
months, I would say,

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:48.520
we've started
adopting Stackdriver.

00:26:48.520 --> 00:26:52.720
Stackdriver provides a nice,
uniform way to do logging.

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:56.240
And we're gradually using
the alerting features more

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:56.740
and more.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:27:00.040
And we can use that on things
like preemptable instances,

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:01.660
which come and go.

00:27:01.660 --> 00:27:03.790
Things like logging
is quite challenging

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:05.980
to figure out good
solutions for.

00:27:05.980 --> 00:27:11.470
But we can also tie that into
Cloud Pub/Sub, to Cloud SQL,

00:27:11.470 --> 00:27:14.320
and to GKE, and we have
a really common way

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:19.810
of querying logs to look for
errors or latency problems,

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:22.260
or just diagnose code.

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:29.520
So what types of models
do we actually produce?

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:32.070
This is a little
bit more information

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:35.950
on the agricultural models
I mentioned earlier.

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:39.090
And we started doing this
in 2015, just a few months

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:40.980
into our company.

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:43.470
How is this done traditionally?

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:46.470
The US Department of
Agriculture will send surveys.

00:27:46.470 --> 00:27:48.660
And they send thousands
of surveys out

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:53.100
to individual farmers a few
times during the growing season

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:56.490
to get progress on the
actual growth of crops.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:58.320
And then they aggregate
the surveys back,

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:01.900
and finally release a number
and then the market moves.

00:28:01.900 --> 00:28:03.150
How do we do it?

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:05.410
We have a quite
different approach.

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:08.590
We pull in about four
quadrillion pixels.

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:12.690
So you can think of billions
and billions of iPhone photos.

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:15.990
And we see every
field, everywhere

00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:18.490
in the Earth, every single day.

00:28:18.490 --> 00:28:20.970
And those are inputs
into our models

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:24.900
to basically predict how
much food will be produced

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:27.370
at the end of the year.

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:30.790
And so as I said, we've iterated
on those models many times.

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:34.300
And you can see
in this graph, we

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:38.290
can show that we not only
are more accurate than the US

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:41.350
Department of Agriculture, but
we get that information much

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:42.380
earlier.

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:44.830
And we can see how things
like a big weather event,

00:28:44.830 --> 00:28:46.630
like a hailstorm in Iowa--

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:48.380
what was the actual
effect of that?

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:54.150
I should say, in
2017, our models

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:59.220
will now be backtested
over the last 13 years.

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:02.400
So we can go back 12 years
ago, or five years ago,

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:04.890
and say, how did the model
perform given this weather

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:05.490
parameter?

00:29:08.210 --> 00:29:11.510
One really exciting
advance is, we're now

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:14.060
extending the use of
these models to do

00:29:14.060 --> 00:29:16.940
things like try and
detect early indications

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:18.900
of a famine outbreak.

00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:20.900
So this is something
in North Africa,

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:24.800
you can think of things
like political instability

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:29.720
that occur from food shortage,
getting aid to places faster,

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:31.280
and the amount of
lives you can save.

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:35.420
It's really exciting
to be a part of that.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:37.360
So next, I'd like to
show you what we just

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:41.860
released on Tuesday, something
called Geovisual Search.

00:29:41.860 --> 00:29:46.990
And Geovisual Search extends
on top of the machine learning

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:49.210
platform that I was
showing you, being

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:53.350
able to do geospatial
queries over windows of time

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:56.290
and pull together multiple
different bands of light.

00:29:56.290 --> 00:29:58.360
Not just always
human-visible bands,

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:03.160
such as red, green, blue, but
also things like infrared.

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:07.020
And build models, and
iterate on models,

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:11.130
to see things that you could
never actually humanly hire

00:30:11.130 --> 00:30:13.650
enough people to see.

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:16.480
As part of that, we
need training data.

00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:19.560
And we have many sources
of training data,

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:21.210
like I mentioned before.

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:25.860
One of those that's open
source is OpenStreetMaps.

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:29.130
We can use a area
such as OpenStreetMaps

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:32.130
to train in a given
geographic location.

00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:35.010
What are we actually
seeing in these images?

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:38.730
And then we use
TensorFlow to build models

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:42.180
that, as we processed our
imagery through there,

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:45.900
we determine various
signatures of each scene

00:30:45.900 --> 00:30:47.610
all across the globe.

00:30:47.610 --> 00:30:52.080
And then we try and see where
else in trillions and trillions

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:54.930
of pixels across the
entire Earth, where

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:58.050
else do we see similar items?

00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:02.780
So this is, again, we use
Pub/Sub in this scenario

00:31:02.780 --> 00:31:04.310
to persist that data.

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:07.040
But this time, we're persisting
the model results out

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:08.540
into BigTable.

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:11.090
And then finally
using Container Engine

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:14.870
to serve the APIs and
the front end interface.

00:31:14.870 --> 00:31:17.120
So, as part of this
talk, I thought

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:19.160
I would just
quickly show you one

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:21.710
problem I would have
on traditionally

00:31:21.710 --> 00:31:22.670
using this approach.

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:24.980
Again, things like
OpenStreetMaps.

00:31:24.980 --> 00:31:27.230
I'm just trying to
train a data set.

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:29.510
It's a public open
source data set.

00:31:29.510 --> 00:31:32.030
Sometimes it's accurate,
sometimes it's not.

00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:34.430
Sometimes our models that
we'll train out of that

00:31:34.430 --> 00:31:36.350
aren't going to produce
that good of results,

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:38.490
and we'll need to try
a different approach.

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:41.640
So the end goal is not to build
a long production database

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:42.140
always.

00:31:42.140 --> 00:31:45.050
It's to get a geospatial
data set in there

00:31:45.050 --> 00:31:47.450
so we can use it for
training, and then

00:31:47.450 --> 00:31:50.740
move on to the next models.

00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:53.260
So, this is-- I don't
know if anyone here

00:31:53.260 --> 00:31:56.620
has had fun with OpenStreetMaps
and using the tool

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:59.140
OSM to PGSQL.

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:01.780
It's an importer
tool that lets you

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:06.250
import all of OpenStreetMaps
into a PostGIS

00:32:06.250 --> 00:32:08.000
compatible database.

00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:11.620
So, as you might be able
to scroll down and see

00:32:11.620 --> 00:32:16.570
all these fun messages, after
a few hours of importing I

00:32:16.570 --> 00:32:20.770
took a 50-gig compressed
OpenStreetMaps file,

00:32:20.770 --> 00:32:23.060
and it's a 50-gig
compressed XML file.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:25.840
So it's really hard to
guess exactly how big that's

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:29.140
going to be in PostGIS.

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:31.210
And all of a sudden,
I get to the spot

00:32:31.210 --> 00:32:34.180
where I have no disk
space left on device

00:32:34.180 --> 00:32:35.860
and I've wasted my time.

00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:41.200
And I need to now make a
bigger VM with a larger disk

00:32:41.200 --> 00:32:42.490
and try this again.

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:44.680
And maybe I've guessed
right the second time.

00:32:44.680 --> 00:32:48.070
But that's not that different
than getting requirements.

00:32:48.070 --> 00:32:51.700
I don't know if any of you have
asked for requirements early

00:32:51.700 --> 00:32:55.360
on in a project, and
you've received input.

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:59.520
And either you never,
ever, used all the space

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:03.130
that was asked for,
or you basically

00:33:03.130 --> 00:33:05.470
started way too small,
and your project

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:07.720
became wildly
successful, and you

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:11.800
needed to import all these other
data sets into the database.

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:15.560
So, one of the features Brett
mentioned was the auto growth.

00:33:15.560 --> 00:33:17.710
So here's an example of--

00:33:17.710 --> 00:33:22.600
by clicking the Auto Growth,
or selecting that in the API,

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:28.360
we could start with a 10-gig
disk and instantly grow.

00:33:28.360 --> 00:33:33.990
And we grew, over a
few hours, to 380--

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:36.400
it looks like 387 gigs.

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:39.670
And the awesome
part about this was

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:43.360
I just am paying for the
amount of storage I actually

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:44.980
need at that given time.

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:48.820
I'm not overestimating,
I'm not underestimating,

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:50.290
and I know it can grow.

00:33:50.290 --> 00:33:53.050
And we're really excited about
things like read replicas

00:33:53.050 --> 00:33:55.180
and high availability as well.

00:33:55.180 --> 00:33:58.330
Especially applying that to
micro services, which sometimes

00:33:58.330 --> 00:34:00.710
start really small,
but then get really,

00:34:00.710 --> 00:34:05.320
really useful as
you add features.

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:09.420
So at this point, I would like
to switch over to the demo.

00:34:09.420 --> 00:34:13.179
And it looks like I need
to type my password.

00:34:13.179 --> 00:34:15.580
And I would like
to show you what

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:24.820
we built on top of Cloud SQL,
PostGIS, BigTable, Pub/Sub.

00:34:24.820 --> 00:34:26.250
This is San Francisco.

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:29.230
This is using the
national aerial imagery.

00:34:29.230 --> 00:34:32.050
We are storing all of this data.

00:34:32.050 --> 00:34:35.620
We are not attaching
PD Disks to VMs.

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:38.800
Instead, we're using
managed instance groups

00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:42.610
which are natively
reading Cloud Storage

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:45.679
with a super fast layer.

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:51.070
So this is all of the US,
and this is all native Cloud

00:34:51.070 --> 00:34:54.520
Storage, reading
to those imagery.

00:34:54.520 --> 00:34:57.250
So, as I mentioned
before-- and by the way,

00:34:57.250 --> 00:35:00.970
if you're really interested
in how we did all of that,

00:35:00.970 --> 00:35:05.410
our CTO will be giving a
talk this afternoon at 5:20,

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:08.320
and that's IO242,
and we'll talk all

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:13.330
about the file system
and Cloud Storage

00:35:13.330 --> 00:35:17.830
as a scalable file system.

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:21.330
So in this example, I
can look for a number

00:35:21.330 --> 00:35:22.800
of different
signatures that we've

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:25.350
trained TensorFlow models on.

00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:28.840
Some of the examples, we have
a center pivot irrigation.

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:34.590
Here's floating top oil storage
tanks, orchards, marinas.

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:38.430
And in this example, I'm going
to look at wind turbines.

00:35:38.430 --> 00:35:42.670
And what we do is, we
slice this entire layer.

00:35:42.670 --> 00:35:45.840
This layer is specifically
aerial imagery.

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:47.460
And this is only of
the United States.

00:35:47.460 --> 00:35:50.790
But then we have other layers
at different resolutions

00:35:50.790 --> 00:35:53.910
and different bands of
light, such as the Landsat

00:35:53.910 --> 00:35:55.560
I mentioned earlier.

00:35:55.560 --> 00:35:59.040
And also Planet, who will
be giving the keynote--

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:01.740
part of the keynote tomorrow.

00:36:01.740 --> 00:36:04.200
And we can search over
all of those layers,

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:09.940
we slice up into tiny squares
and we write a signature.

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:13.680
And this is all done
through TensorFlow models.

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:18.970
And then, if we
click on this area,

00:36:18.970 --> 00:36:22.840
I can see all of the
different places on this map

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:27.830
that we've found a
visually similar object.

00:36:27.830 --> 00:36:32.410
So here, I can scroll
in and I can see,

00:36:32.410 --> 00:36:36.400
and it looks like we've found
visually similar wind turbines.

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:42.550
I can click over to
somewhere in California,

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:46.720
and again, I can
find wind turbines.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:50.920
Pretty interesting use case
is center pivot irrigation.

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:53.450
And again, I'll look
over all of the Earth.

00:36:53.450 --> 00:36:54.760
And you think, how many people?

00:36:54.760 --> 00:36:57.760
I could hire 10,000
people and have them just

00:36:57.760 --> 00:37:00.670
looking at scenes every
day as they come in.

00:37:00.670 --> 00:37:03.670
They would probably commit
suicide or something else,

00:37:03.670 --> 00:37:08.830
but in this case, we
can easily use things

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:12.250
like machine learning to scale
up to levels that you really

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:13.540
never could humanly.

00:37:13.540 --> 00:37:16.150
So here, we've found
more crop circles.

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:18.940
And finally, just an
example on Planet.

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:21.520
Planet's a really neat data set.

00:37:21.520 --> 00:37:25.735
Planet is not launching
hundreds of millions

00:37:25.735 --> 00:37:27.880
of dollar large
satellites the way

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:31.010
NASA would have traditionally
launched satellites.

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:34.500
They're instead using really
small, commodity-based cube

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:35.350
sats.

00:37:35.350 --> 00:37:38.150
And they'll see the
whole Earth once a day

00:37:38.150 --> 00:37:40.930
at three meters for each pixel.

00:37:40.930 --> 00:37:42.910
And so with Planet,
we have a number

00:37:42.910 --> 00:37:46.540
of things in this China
dataset that we've trained on.

00:37:46.540 --> 00:37:50.560
And here's one example
of solar farms in China.

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:53.440
And I can look
everywhere in China,

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:58.000
and I can see different places
in China that are solar farms.

00:37:58.000 --> 00:38:00.130
And here is all
the other results

00:38:00.130 --> 00:38:05.720
that we found that are the
top 500 most similar searches.

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:08.290
So if I could switch
back to the presentation.

00:38:15.850 --> 00:38:18.640
So, where we're going with
this is things like eventually

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:23.860
being able to see, in 2011 I
didn't see a wind turbine here.

00:38:23.860 --> 00:38:25.990
And now as we've
brought new imagery,

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:28.540
we all the sudden see
things like, oh, there's

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:29.840
a wind turbine.

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:33.130
This is really neat
type of capability.

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:35.765
So as we're streaming
in data from all

00:38:35.765 --> 00:38:38.140
of these different satellites,
looking down at the Earth,

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:41.050
we're constantly feeding
those back into models

00:38:41.050 --> 00:38:44.860
and aggregating results that
help our customers really

00:38:44.860 --> 00:38:48.010
answer hard problems.

00:38:48.010 --> 00:38:51.280
Just two other
real brief examples

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:54.070
of the types of
scenarios our platform

00:38:54.070 --> 00:38:58.230
has to adjust for, and how
there's not just one solution.

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:02.080
In this example, with red,
green, and blue imagery,

00:39:02.080 --> 00:39:04.630
it's quite hard to
actually pick out

00:39:04.630 --> 00:39:07.480
where the wind
turbines actually are.

00:39:07.480 --> 00:39:11.230
However, we can use, from
the European Space Agency's

00:39:11.230 --> 00:39:14.770
Sentinel 1 satellite,
there's something

00:39:14.770 --> 00:39:17.680
called synthetic aperture
radar on the satellite.

00:39:17.680 --> 00:39:21.070
And we collect this
globally, and--

00:39:21.070 --> 00:39:25.630
excuse me-- synthetic aperture
radar emits and detects radar

00:39:25.630 --> 00:39:26.260
signals.

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:29.380
Those radar signals can
pass through clouds.

00:39:29.380 --> 00:39:31.750
They can even work at night.

00:39:31.750 --> 00:39:35.410
And it gives you a measurement
of almost like a height

00:39:35.410 --> 00:39:37.420
measurement on the ground.

00:39:37.420 --> 00:39:40.750
And you can see these
vertical lines up and down,

00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:46.300
showing all of those wind
turbines in that field.

00:39:46.300 --> 00:39:50.290
More on the agriculture side.

00:39:50.290 --> 00:39:52.510
Here it's quite
challenging to actually see

00:39:52.510 --> 00:39:55.570
the Ove River in Russia.

00:39:55.570 --> 00:39:59.020
You can see it's kind of dark,
and there's definitely an area

00:39:59.020 --> 00:40:02.330
that you think the river
and vegetation's there.

00:40:02.330 --> 00:40:06.130
However, if we switch over
to red edge bands that

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:09.940
are more like infrared,
you can see the river

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:12.640
and the vegetation is
highlighted in really, really

00:40:12.640 --> 00:40:13.870
bright yellow.

00:40:13.870 --> 00:40:16.840
And then you can
see water channels.

00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:18.430
So there's not one solution.

00:40:18.430 --> 00:40:20.440
Our platform needed
to be able to handle

00:40:20.440 --> 00:40:23.740
a lot of different types of
machine learning questions

00:40:23.740 --> 00:40:26.440
that we were asking of it.

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:30.640
And not every problem needs
the same bands of light

00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:35.560
or the same types of
temporal cadences.

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:37.570
And finally, I'd
just like to wrap up.

00:40:37.570 --> 00:40:40.060
Where we're going
at Descartes is

00:40:40.060 --> 00:40:44.740
moving on to using things,
taking this platform that we've

00:40:44.740 --> 00:40:47.830
built for these
internal use cases,

00:40:47.830 --> 00:40:51.110
and making this
commercially available.

00:40:51.110 --> 00:40:53.410
So we have all of
this imagery available

00:40:53.410 --> 00:40:56.320
that's been cleaned
and co-registered

00:40:56.320 --> 00:40:59.170
and has a really nice
API to do analysis

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:03.940
across many different sources
just with the basic Python API.

00:41:03.940 --> 00:41:07.120
And we hope that showing
you these types of things,

00:41:07.120 --> 00:41:09.970
you have ideas of scenarios
I haven't shown you.

00:41:09.970 --> 00:41:13.840
And you would like to maybe
build models on your own,

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:17.020
